# Read by sast-engine's BaseAgent._loadIgnorePatterns() (sast-engine/agents/base-agent.js),
# applied as fast-glob ignores in discoverFiles(). One pattern per line; a trailing "/" means
# "this directory and everything under it". Blank lines and "#" comments are skipped.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#
# This repository contains a pattern-matching security scanner, so scanning it with itself
# produces a large number of findings that are not vulnerabilities: the rule definitions are
# made of the very strings the rules look for. The purest example is auth-bypass-agent.js,
# where these two lines sit next to each other:
#
#     title: 'Django: DEBUG = True',
#     regex: /\bDEBUG\s*=\s*True\b/g,
#
# The regex matches the title, so the rule reports itself as a high-severity finding. The same
# happens to doc comments listing what an agent detects and to `description:` strings
# explaining a pattern. BaseAgent.scanFileWithPatterns() tests every regex against every
# physical line with no notion of whether that line is code, a comment, or a string literal —
# so any file that *describes* a vulnerability pattern also *matches* it.
#
# Measured on this repo: 107 of 134 deterministic findings came from the two directories below,
# and every one of the 40 that reached the LLM adjudication pass was closed as a false positive.
#
# SCOPE: THE RULE CORPUS ONLY, NOT THE WHOLE ENGINE
#
# Only the pattern-definition directories are excluded. sast-engine/enumerate.js,
# ast-extract.js, remediation-apply.js and commands/ are ordinary application code that can
# hold ordinary bugs — remediation-apply.js in particular is the one function in this app that
# writes to disk — so they stay in scope deliberately. Do not widen this to "sast-engine/".
#
# This only affects the deterministic pattern engine's file discovery. The reasoning passes get
# the target directory as cwd and read it with their own Read/Grep/Glob tools, so they still
# see these files and can still report real logic bugs in them.
#
# Trade-off worth knowing: base-agent.js and orchestrator.js are real logic that lives in
# agents/, so the pattern engine no longer scans them either. Both produced zero findings, and
# the reasoning half still covers them — but that is the cost of a directory-level exclusion.
#
# KNOWN SIDE EFFECT: WHOLE-REPO ANALYSES SEE A SMALLER REPO
#
# Any rule that reasons about the *whole tree* rather than one line now works from a partial
# picture, because usage that lives in an excluded file is invisible to it. One case is live
# and expected: UNUSED_DEPENDENCY reports `fast-glob` as unused. It is not — it is imported by
# base-agent.js, model-scan-agent.js, memory-poisoning-agent.js, install-guard-agent.js and
# agent-config-scanner.js, every one of which sits under the excluded agents/ directory. That
# finding is a false positive created by this file, not a real one; treat a low-severity
# UNUSED_DEPENDENCY hit here with suspicion before acting on it.
#
# The same caution applies to UnusedGuardAgent's reference counting: a function defined in a
# scanned file but only called from an excluded one would look like it has zero call sites.
# Nothing in this repo currently hits that, but it is the same failure mode.

sast-engine/agents/
sast-engine/utils/

# Golden-master test input, not application code. test/fixtures/vuln-app/ is a deliberately
# insecure miniature Express app that exists so the deterministic engine's output can be
# snapshotted and compared across a refactor. It is never imported by the app and never
# executed. If a real scan of this repo ever reports a finding from test/fixtures/, this
# exclusion has regressed — see test/fixtures/vuln-app/README.md.
test/fixtures/

# Deliberately NOT excluded: *.md. REASONING_ENGINE.md contributes 3 secret-pattern findings
# that are documentation examples, but excluding markdown from a secret scanner is a bad
# default — a credential really committed into a doc is a real finding, and this is exactly
# the case GitHistoryScanner and SecretsAgent exist to catch.
